Second commit, on a point the first one made worse.

`OidcSignatureVerifier` already declared `ClockInterface $clock = new Clock()`, and this pull request added the same default to `OidcLoginAuthenticator` and to the new `OidcTokenRefresher`. `symfony/clock` is a development dependency of `symfony/security-http`, so building one of these classes without the argument, outside of `security-bundle`, died on an unknown class rather than saying what to install.

The argument is nullable now, and the three constructors check the component before falling back to it, the way `DateType` and its siblings do for `DatePoint`:

```
The "symfony/clock" component is required to build "Symfony\Component\Security\Http\Authenticator\Oidc\OidcTokenRefresher" without a clock. Try running "composer require symfony/clock", or pass any PSR-20 clock to the constructor.
```

Making `symfony/clock` a hard requirement of `security-http` was the other option. It was dropped: it would install the component for every application, including those whose firewall never touches OIDC.

Nothing changes for `security-bundle`, which requires `symfony/clock` and injects the `clock` service.

There is a neighbouring gap this pull request does not close. `symfony/security-http` does not require `psr/clock` either, while `OidcIdToken`, `OidcSignatureVerifier`, `OidcLoginAuthenticator` and `OidcTokenRefresher` all type-hint `Psr\Clock\ClockInterface`. That one is not the same trade-off, since `psr/clock` holds a single interface and no code, so requiring it costs nothing. Say the word and I add the line here, or send it separately.

