Pushed two fixes. The lazy-firewall problem you fixed yourself before I got there was the important one, and the `supports(): ?bool` returning null is the right shape.

**The PKCE guard was fail-open.** `'authorization_code' === $body['grant_type']` refuses only that one value, so `grant_type` absent, null, empty, `Authorization_Code` or an array all skipped the verifier, and an absent key also raised an undefined-key warning. Not reachable today, since both call sites pass a string literal, but `applyClientAuthentication()` is `abstract protected` and is the documented way to add a client type, so a grant added later would have reached the token endpoint unprotected. Inverted to `'refresh_token' !== ($body['grant_type'] ?? null)`, which refuses every grant but the one that genuinely carries no verifier.

**The `sub` check was skipped when there was no previous ID token.** `verifyRefreshedIdToken()` returned early when the security token carried none, so a refreshed ID token for any subject was stored:

```
previous id_token = NULL  =>  ACCEPTED, stored sub="attacker-99"
previous id_token = ''    =>  ACCEPTED, stored sub="attacker-99"
```

Unreachable through `oidc_login`, since `exchangeAuthorizationCode()` refuses a response without a non-empty `id_token`, but reachable for the on-demand use your description documents, on a token an application assembled itself. It throws now, with a test that revert-verifies.

For the record, everything else the description claims verified. Every §12.2 check exists and rejects: forged signature, a key outside the JWKS, wrong `iss`, wrong `aud`, expired, missing `exp`, future `iat`, missing `iat`, future `nbf`, and a valid ID token for another subject. After each rejection all four token attributes are byte-identical to what they were, so "leaves the security token exactly as it was" holds. Rotation holds both ways, and the error classification never turns a retryable failure into a permanent one.

Two things worth a sentence in the description rather than code: the refresher uses the global `http_client`, so a slow provider blocks the request and there is no per-firewall timeout; and `setToken(null)` on `invalid_grant` is a deauthentication, not a logout, so no `LogoutEvent` fires and a `remember_me` cookie re-logs the user in on the next request with no refresh token.

Security/Http 985, SecurityBundle 561, both green.
